1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,752
The Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale is a structured observation
scale developed for describing agitation and sedation. The AET module
introduces it for prehospital assessment, but a score does not
identify why the patient is agitated or less responsive.

2
00:00:16,752 --> 00:00:29,640
Use this Week 10 supplement as shared vocabulary only when the course
or current service protocol calls for it. Never let a scale delay
immediate treatment of an airway, breathing or circulation threat.

3
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:44,760
The scale ranges from plus four, combative, through zero, alert and
calm, to minus five, unarousable. The negative anchors distinguish eye
contact, voice response and response to physical stimulation.

4
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:58,752
Follow the exact scoring method taught by your program and any current
local protocol. This overview is not an independent bedside scoring
credential or an instruction to apply physical stimulation outside
training.

5
00:00:58,752 --> 00:01:10,176
Zero means alert and calm. Plus one describes restlessness without
aggressive or vigorous movement; it is not a score for every person
who reports feeling anxious.

6
00:01:10,176 --> 00:01:25,080
Plus two describes frequent non-purposeful movement. The original ICU
scale also includes fighting the ventilator. That example does not
require an EMS patient to be ventilated before the behavioural anchor
can be used.

7
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:39,888
Plus three includes pulling out a tube or catheter or aggressive
behaviour. Plus four describes overt combative or violent conduct with
immediate danger. Use the actual pattern, not how much the clinician
dislikes the person’s words.

8
00:01:39,888 --> 00:01:54,216
A score describes observed behaviour, not intent or a legal
conclusion. Scene-safety decisions and medical assessment still
require their own evidence; a positive score never creates automatic
restraint permission.

9
00:01:54,216 --> 00:02:07,056
If the person is not alert, address them by name and ask them to open
their eyes and look toward you. Observe whether they awaken and make
eye contact; eye opening by itself is not the same finding.

10
00:02:07,056 --> 00:02:22,104
Minus one is drowsiness with sustained awakening and eye contact to
voice. Minus two is brief awakening with eye contact. The school and
the SCCM chart contrast more than ten seconds with less than ten
seconds.

11
00:02:22,104 --> 00:02:40,008
The VCU web table uses ten seconds or more for minus one, while its
introduction also contains an apparent plus-five range typo
contradicted by its own table. Use the adopted chart and preserve the
difference instead of inventing a universal boundary or a plus-five
score.

12
00:02:40,008 --> 00:02:56,424
In rehearsal, use clear examples: six seconds of eye contact after the
prompt fits minus two; fourteen seconds fits minus one under both
wordings. At a boundary, record the observed duration and apply the
complete local version.

13
00:02:56,424 --> 00:03:09,600
A movement or eye opening caused by the verbal prompt, without eye
contact, fits minus three. Do not call that no response merely because
the person does not speak or obey a complex instruction.

14
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:24,480
If there is no voice response, the approved physical step
distinguishes minus four from minus five. A response in that step
supports minus four; no response to either voice or the properly
performed physical assessment supports minus five.

15
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:41,664
The exact stimulus must follow current training and the patient
context. The school mentions shoulder, jaw and orbital methods; the
historical SCCM method and Northern Health palliative adaptation
differ. Do not combine these into a new painful routine.

16
00:03:41,664 --> 00:04:00,048
Northern Health explicitly advises against sternal rubbing in
palliative assessment. If stimulation is unsafe, inappropriate or not
performed, document the limit and obtain assessment rather than
manufacturing a complete score. Immediate life-threat care must not
wait for scale completion.

17
00:04:00,048 --> 00:04:13,656
A reproducible method makes comparisons more useful. Record the time,
what prompt was used, what the patient did and what else was
happening, such as pain, hypoxia concern, a recent medication or a
seizure.

18
00:04:13,656 --> 00:04:24,192
Do not score from a label such as “sleepy” or “aggressive” alone.
Describe the actual response and document whether the patient’s state
changed after an intervention.

19
00:04:24,192 --> 00:04:36,336
Begin with spontaneous observation. If an alert and calm or positive
agitation anchor is established, no additional stimulation is needed
merely to finish three steps.

20
00:04:36,336 --> 00:04:51,840
For a person who is not alert, use the verbal assessment and observe
awakening, eye contact and its duration. A voice response can
establish minus one, minus two or minus three without proceeding to a
physical stimulus.

21
00:04:51,840 --> 00:05:03,312
Only absent voice response takes the scoring sequence toward the
approved physical step. Follow the taught method and stop if the
clinical context requires a different urgent response.

22
00:05:03,312 --> 00:05:17,760
Record the score together with the method and time. A short sequence
improves consistency only when its branches are respected; applying
every stimulus to every patient is neither necessary nor faithful to
the tool.

23
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:35,040
A person may have a low score because of fatigue, medication, hypoxia,
head injury, seizure, low glucose or another cause. A high score may
reflect pain, fear, delirium, intoxication, hypoxia or a threat
response.

24
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:49,176
The scale cannot determine whether a person has decision-making
capacity, whether a refusal is valid, or whether a restraint is
permitted. Those questions require the relevant law, current pathway
and full assessment.

25
00:05:49,176 --> 00:06:02,688
Before interpreting absent voice response, consider whether the prompt
was accessible. Hearing loss, language mismatch or severe
environmental noise can prevent the expected response without proving
deeper sedation.

26
00:06:02,688 --> 00:06:17,400
Eye injury or visual impairment can limit the eye-contact observation.
Movement limitation or paralysis can make a motor response
unavailable. Do not turn an unobservable response into a negative
finding automatically.

27
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:30,240
Describe the prompt, what occurred and why interpretation is limited.
Seek an appropriate alternative clinical assessment under the current
pathway instead of inventing a replacement RASS formula.

28
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:43,128
A neurological examination, delirium assessment, capacity decision and
airway evaluation answer different questions. The score can contribute
shared language, but it does not replace those assessments.

29
00:06:43,128 --> 00:06:57,792
If a patient becomes difficult to arouse, assess for immediate life
threats before discussing scale trends. Preserve access to the airway
and chest, check breathing and perfusion, and use your current PCP
pathway.

30
00:06:57,792 --> 00:07:07,896
Repeat measurements with the same method when possible, but do not
wait for a second score if the patient is collapsing, hypoventilating
or cannot protect the airway.

31
00:07:07,896 --> 00:07:22,056
A trend is useful only when linked to clinical context. Ask about new
medication, dose changes, substances, recent illness, head impact, a
witnessed seizure, severe pain and the patient’s baseline.

32
00:07:22,056 --> 00:07:34,008
A behavioral label must not hide physical deterioration. Conversely, a
medical assessment should not erase the patient’s fear, goals or right
to be treated respectfully.

33
00:07:34,008 --> 00:07:48,408
Compare the score with respiratory effort, voice, eye opening,
orientation, temperature, perfusion and the overall examination. A
score that improves while breathing worsens is not an improvement in
the whole patient.

34
00:07:48,408 --> 00:08:00,360
Use trends to communicate urgency and response, not to create false
precision. If the patient has a new decline, report the time course
and escalate according to the current service pathway.

35
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:13,944
In an original assessment example, a person awakens to their name and
makes eye contact for six seconds. The observed anchor is minus two;
it is not minus three because eye contact did occur.

36
00:08:13,944 --> 00:08:27,624
Later, using the same accessible prompt, eye contact lasts fourteen
seconds. That observation fits minus one. Report the measured change
and context, not simply that the number is better.

37
00:08:27,624 --> 00:08:40,248
A different patient moves an arm to voice but never makes eye contact.
That fits the minus-three response pattern if the assessment is
interpretable. A spoken answer is not required for that anchor.

38
00:08:40,248 --> 00:08:54,816
Check physiology alongside every comparison. If breathing effort or
perfusion is worsening, a more awake appearance does not establish
overall recovery. Treat the clinical change according to the current
service pathway.

39
00:08:54,816 --> 00:09:10,584
Sedating medication can change respiratory drive, airway protection,
blood pressure and responsiveness. Effects may combine with illness,
alcohol or other substances, so deterioration must be actively sought.

40
00:09:10,584 --> 00:09:25,728
This course does not give medication doses or authorize PCP chemical
restraint. Administer, assist with or monitor medication only as
permitted by your licence, training, current service protocol and
instructor direction.

41
00:09:25,728 --> 00:09:38,256
A good report avoids “RASS is minus three” as the only information.
Add the actual stimulus and response, vital-sign trend, relevant
history and the action taken.

42
00:09:38,256 --> 00:09:49,032
Say what remains uncertain and what should be watched next. Keep the
handover focused on patient safety, not on defending a label or a
previous decision.

43
00:09:49,032 --> 00:10:02,880
An original report could begin: At this assessment the patient opened
their eyes to their name and held eye contact for six seconds,
matching minus two on the chart in use. This is more informative than
sleepy.

44
00:10:02,880 --> 00:10:16,008
Continue with the clinical findings you actually assessed and their
trend. Add relevant medication or exposure history if known; do not
imply that a negative RASS proves the person received a sedative.

45
00:10:16,008 --> 00:10:27,840
State the action and response, including any inability to complete a
component. Do not chart normal breathing, capacity or a physical-
stimulus response that was not evaluated.

46
00:10:27,840 --> 00:10:38,592
Finish with what remains concerning and who receives responsibility
for follow-up. A complete sentence about the person is safer than
handing over only a signed number.
